The '''Transportation Workers Displacement Fund Addendum''' was a bill introduced by the [[Government of Burawa]] on 8 August 2026 to amend the existing [[Transportation Workers Displacement Fund]] to include taxiworkers among its eligible categories. The bill was introduced in response to the [[2026 Taxiworkers Union protest|August 2026 Taxiworkers Union protest]], a nationwide work stoppage by the [[Taxiworkers Union of Burawa]] triggered by the deployment of the [[WhA Locus]] autonomous robotaxi.

The bill passed the [[Senate of Burawa|Senate]] on 11 August 2026 by a vote of 131 to 99 but failed in the [[Lower House of Burawa|Lower House]] on 12 August 2026 by a vote of 312 to 484. The failure was the result of opposition and internal division across multiple parties, with differing objections raised by budget-conscious members of the [[Socialist Party of Burawa|Socialist Party]], the [[Industrial Party of Burawa|Industrial Party]], the [[Forward Party of Burawa|Forward Party]], further-left parties, and a split within the [[National Party of Burawa|National Party]].

== Background ==

The [[Transportation Workers Displacement Fund]] was established in 2022 to provide financial support to workers in the transportation sector affected by automation. The fund was created specifically for passenger train drivers, who were scheduled to be phased out by 2035 as part of the transition to automated train operations. The fund's design reflected a defined, time-bound automation programme with a clear displacement horizon. Taxiworkers were not included among the fund's eligible categories at the time of its establishment, as no comparable phase-out timeline existed for taxi services.

The deployment of the [[WhA Locus]] autonomous robotaxi in August 2026 brought this gap to national attention. Following the [[2026 Taxiworkers Union protest|August 2026 Taxiworkers Union protest]], which began on 1 August, [[Leader of the Burawi Republic|Leader]] [[Muna Xasan]] called on the [[Parliament of Burawa|Parliament]] to accelerate legislation to extend the fund's coverage to taxiworkers.

== Provisions ==

The bill proposed to amend the Transportation Workers Displacement Fund to include taxiworkers as an eligible category. Under the bill's provisions, the fund would fully fund the salaries and pensions of taxiworkers who had worked in the industry for at least four years. Workers meeting the tenure requirement would receive continued salary payments and pension contributions from the fund following displacement by autonomous vehicle deployment.

The four-year tenure requirement was a notable limitation of the bill, as it excluded taxiworkers with fewer than four years of service from the fund's protections. This threshold became a point of contention during the legislative debate.

A central argument raised against the bill was that the government did not intend to reduce taxiworker employment in the near term. The WhA Locus deployment was limited to 150 vehicles in a single metropolitan area, with no announced timeline for a broader phase-out of conventional taxi services. Opponents argued that adding taxiworkers to the displacement fund would expand the fund's obligations to cover a category of workers whose displacement was not imminent, unlike the train drivers for whom the fund was originally designed and whose phase-out by 2035 was already legislated.

== Legislative history ==

=== Introduction ===

The bill was drafted and introduced on 8 August 2026, one week after the start of the [[2026 Taxiworkers Union protest|August 2026 Taxiworkers Union protest]]. The government, led by [[Muna Xasan]] of the [[Party for Equitable Progress]] (PEP), sought to address the protest through legislative action rather than by invoking the Emergency Workforce Authorization Act of 1964.

=== Senate vote (11 August) ===

The bill was brought to a vote in the [[Senate of Burawa|Senate]] on 11 August 2026. It passed by a vote of 131 to 99, achieving a simple majority of the 230-seat chamber. The Senate vote indicated cross-party support for the principle of extending displacement protections to taxiworkers, though the narrower margin suggested concerns about the bill's specific provisions.

=== Lower House vote (12 August) ===

The bill proceeded to the [[Lower House of Burawa|Lower House]] on 12 August 2026, where it failed by a vote of 312 to 484. The failure was the result of opposition and division across multiple parties, each with distinct objections to the bill.

== Party positions ==

=== Socialist Party ===

The [[Socialist Party of Burawa|Socialist Party]], the largest party in the [[Lower House of Burawa|Lower House]], was internally divided on the bill. Three factions within the party emerged during the debate:

Budget-conscious members opposed the bill on fiscal grounds, arguing that fully funding salaries and pensions for eligible taxiworkers represented a significant and open-ended expenditure commitment, particularly given that no near-term reduction in taxiworker employment was planned. A second faction aligned with the [[Industrial Party of Burawa|Industrial Party]]'s position, arguing that displaced taxiworkers could be reassigned to new state-sector employment through existing reworking programmes rather than receiving funded salaries and pensions. A third faction supported the bill as proposed.

The Socialist Party's failure to deliver a unified position was a principal factor in the bill's defeat.

=== Industrial Party ===

The [[Industrial Party of Burawa|Industrial Party]] opposed the bill outright. The party argued that the fund was unnecessary because taxiworkers, as salaried employees of metropolitan administrations, could be reassigned to comparable state-sector positions through existing reworking programmes. The Industrial Party further argued that the displacement fund was designed for workers facing a defined phase-out timeline — as in the case of train drivers scheduled for replacement by 2035 — and that no comparable timeline existed for taxiworkers. Adding taxiworkers to the fund would expand its obligations without a corresponding displacement horizon, making the expenditure difficult to justify.

=== Forward Party ===

The [[Forward Party of Burawa|Forward Party]] proposed an alternative concession that would replace the salary and pension funding provisions with specialised retraining programmes for affected taxiworkers. The party did not support the bill as drafted, arguing that retraining would provide a more sustainable path for displaced workers than direct financial support.

=== Further-left parties ===

Further-left parties, including the [[Communist Party of Burawa|Communist Party]], criticised the bill as insufficiently expansive. These parties objected to the four-year tenure requirement, arguing that it excluded a significant portion of the taxiworker workforce from the fund's protections. Their position was that all taxiworkers should be eligible for the fund regardless of length of service.

=== National Party ===

The [[National Party of Burawa|National Party]] was split between budget conservatives who opposed the expenditure and members who supported the bill to appease the [[Taxiworkers Union of Burawa|Taxiworkers Union]]. The party did not deliver a unified vote.

== Aftermath ==

As of 14 August 2026, the [[2026 Taxiworkers Union protest|August 2026 Taxiworkers Union protest]] remains ongoing. The Xasan government has not indicated whether it will reintroduce the bill, amend its provisions to address the objections raised, or pursue alternative measures to resolve the dispute.

The failure of the bill highlighted the political challenges facing [[Muna Xasan]]'s government. The [[Party for Equitable Progress]] holds only 22 of the 796 seats in the Lower House, requiring Xasan to build coalitions across party lines. The diverse and conflicting objections to the bill — from fiscal conservatives, reworking programme advocates, retraining proponents, and those seeking more expansive coverage — illustrated the difficulty of constructing a legislative majority for the government's agenda.

